1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,583
Lose one piece of data, and two transport
services can react

2
00:00:05,583 --> 00:00:11,167
very differently. One may hold later bytes
until the gap is

3
00:00:11,167 --> 00:00:16,750
repaired; the other may let later messages
through. Which behavior does

4
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:22,333
your application actually need?

5
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:27,483
Start with the destination. IP reaches a
network endpoint; a port

6
00:00:27,483 --> 00:00:32,633
helps identify the application endpoint there.
Both TCP and UDP use

7
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:37,783
port numbers, allowing different conversations
and applications to share the same

8
00:00:37,783 --> 00:00:42,933
networked machine.

9
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:48,525
UDP carries separate datagrams. When a
datagram arrives intact, its boundary

10
00:00:48,525 --> 00:00:54,117
remains visible to the application. UDP itself
doesn't guarantee arrival, order,

11
00:00:54,117 --> 00:00:59,708
or freedom from duplicates. A message boundary
and reliable delivery are

12
00:00:59,708 --> 00:01:05,300
independent properties.

13
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:10,883
TCP presents an ordered byte stream instead.
HEL, LO, and an

14
00:01:10,883 --> 00:01:16,467
exclamation mark form continuous bytes,
without preserving the original write
boundaries.

15
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:22,050
The application must define how to recognize
complete messages within that

16
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:27,633
stream.

17
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:33,000
To begin our TCP example, each endpoint needs
starting sequence information

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,367
and connection state. Three handshake messages
establish that shared state. The

19
00:01:38,367 --> 00:01:43,733
result is a logical connection between
endpoints, rather than a reserved

20
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:49,100
route through the network.

21
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:54,575
The client sends SYN with sequence number one
hundred. SYN occupies

22
00:01:54,575 --> 00:02:00,050
one sequence position, so this side's first
data byte starts at

23
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:05,525
one hundred and one. That detail will make the
following acknowledgment

24
00:02:05,525 --> 00:02:11,000
numbers fit together.

25
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,367
The server answers with SYN at five hundred
and ACK one

26
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:21,733
hundred and one. Two number spaces now appear:
the server's own

27
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:27,100
sequence, and its acknowledgment of the
client. Each direction tracks a

28
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:32,467
separate flow.

29
00:02:32,467 --> 00:02:38,267
The client acknowledges five hundred and one.
Both sides now have

30
00:02:38,267 --> 00:02:44,067
the starting state for this exchange. The
client's first data still

31
00:02:44,067 --> 00:02:49,867
begins at one hundred and one; the handshake
has established how

32
00:02:49,867 --> 00:02:55,667
the counters line up.

33
00:02:55,667 --> 00:03:01,575
Now send one hundred bytes starting at one
hundred and one.

34
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:07,483
They occupy positions through two hundred. TCP
numbers bytes, not whole

35
00:03:07,483 --> 00:03:13,392
packets, so those positions retain their
meaning even when data is

36
00:03:13,392 --> 00:03:19,300
divided into different segment sizes.

37
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:24,883
ACK two hundred and one says the earlier bytes
have arrived

38
00:03:24,883 --> 00:03:30,467
in order, and names the next expected
position. This cumulative boundary

39
00:03:30,467 --> 00:03:36,050
reports the received prefix of the stream,
rather than counting how

40
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:41,633
many packets crossed the screen.

41
00:03:41,633 --> 00:03:47,108
Later bytes arrive while the middle is
missing. The receiver can

42
00:03:47,108 --> 00:03:52,583
buffer positions three hundred and one through
four hundred, but still

43
00:03:52,583 --> 00:03:58,058
expects two hundred and one. Arriving later
isn't enough to advance

44
00:03:58,058 --> 00:04:03,533
the ordered boundary.

45
00:04:03,533 --> 00:04:09,225
Retransmit the missing middle. Once it
arrives, the stored later bytes

46
00:04:09,225 --> 00:04:14,917
connect to the earlier run, and ACK four
hundred and one

47
00:04:14,917 --> 00:04:20,608
can advance. Repairing one gap makes an entire
waiting stretch available

48
00:04:20,608 --> 00:04:26,300
in order.

49
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:31,992
What if an acknowledgment disappears after
successful delivery? The sender may

50
00:04:31,992 --> 00:04:37,683
repeat those bytes. Sequence information lets
the receiver recognize them and

51
00:04:37,683 --> 00:04:43,375
avoid delivering them twice. Lost feedback
creates uncertainty, rather than necessarily

52
00:04:43,375 --> 00:04:49,067
indicating lost data.

53
00:04:49,067 --> 00:04:54,433
The recovered order comes with a cost. Later
bytes may be

54
00:04:54,433 --> 00:04:59,800
physically present while the application waits
for an earlier gap. Buffers

55
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:05,167
hold them, and recovery takes time. This delay
follows from the

56
00:05:05,167 --> 00:05:10,533
service TCP has promised.

57
00:05:10,533 --> 00:05:16,225
Flow control protects the receiver's limited
storage. In a three-hundred-byte buffer,

58
00:05:16,225 --> 00:05:21,917
two hundred occupied bytes leave one hundred
available. Advertising that remaining

59
00:05:21,917 --> 00:05:27,608
room tells the sender how much more it can
accept before

60
00:05:27,608 --> 00:05:33,300
the application consumes data.

61
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:38,558
Fill the remaining space and the receive
window reaches zero. Then

62
00:05:38,558 --> 00:05:43,817
the application reads two hundred bytes,
making space again. A new

63
00:05:43,817 --> 00:05:49,075
advertised window permits more sending.
Receiver capacity changes as the application

64
00:05:49,075 --> 00:05:54,333
processes the stream.

65
00:05:54,333 --> 00:06:00,133
But a receiver with free space can still sit
beyond an

66
00:06:00,133 --> 00:06:05,933
overloaded router. Congestion control
addresses pressure on the path; flow control

67
00:06:05,933 --> 00:06:11,733
addresses receiver space. The sender must
respect both constraints, because they

68
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:17,533
protect different resources.

69
00:06:17,533 --> 00:06:22,900
Now lose a UDP datagram. UDP itself doesn't
retry it or

70
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:28,267
hold later datagrams behind it. The
application sees whatever arrives and

71
00:06:28,267 --> 00:06:33,633
decides whether that missing information
should be recovered, replaced, or simply

72
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:39,000
left behind.

73
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,583
Applications can build retries,
acknowledgments, protection, and congestion
control above UDP.

74
00:06:44,583 --> 00:06:50,167
Those capabilities belong to the protocol
using UDP. Choosing a datagram

75
00:06:50,167 --> 00:06:55,750
service leaves room to define behavior, but
doesn't provide those mechanisms

76
00:06:55,750 --> 00:07:01,333
automatically.

77
00:07:01,333 --> 00:07:06,383
This is why calling UDP universally faster
misses the interesting question.

78
00:07:06,383 --> 00:07:11,433
Delay depends on the application, path, and
recovery rules. Compare what

79
00:07:11,433 --> 00:07:16,483
information must arrive, and when it remains
useful, before comparing the

80
00:07:16,483 --> 00:07:21,533
speed of two protocols.

81
00:07:21,533 --> 00:07:27,225
For a complete file, a missing range leaves
the result incomplete.

82
00:07:27,225 --> 00:07:32,917
TCP's reliable ordered stream fits that
requirement. The application still handles

83
00:07:32,917 --> 00:07:38,608
its own message framing and connection
failures; a transport service doesn't

84
00:07:38,608 --> 00:07:44,300
remove every possible failure.

85
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:49,883
For some live updates, a newer position makes
an older missing

86
00:07:49,883 --> 00:07:55,467
position less useful. Waiting to recover
history may hurt the experience.

87
00:07:55,467 --> 00:08:01,050
That's an application-specific choice, not a
blanket guarantee that UDP delivers

88
00:08:01,050 --> 00:08:06,633
fresher information.

89
00:08:06,633 --> 00:08:12,108
A TCP connection also closes through
coordinated state changes. Each direction

90
00:08:12,108 --> 00:08:17,583
can end separately, and FIN occupies a
sequence position. Acknowledgments confirm

91
00:08:17,583 --> 00:08:23,058
the close. The diagram shows one path through
that process, rather

92
00:08:23,058 --> 00:08:28,533
than every possible timing.

93
00:08:28,533 --> 00:08:33,900
Return to the lost piece. Is completeness
essential, is order required,

94
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:39,267
or can newer information replace it? TCP and
UDP offer different

95
00:08:39,267 --> 00:08:44,633
starting services. The useful choice follows
from what the application needs

96
00:08:44,633 --> 00:08:50,000
its receiver to know.
